# Line-ending and diff rules for this repository.
#
# Goal: the module must load and behave identically under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (Windows only) and
# PowerShell 7.x+ on Windows, Linux, and macOS, no matter what each contributor's core.autocrlf is set to.
#
# Line endings do not affect how PowerShell parses a script (every supported host reads LF and CRLF
# equally), so these rules exist to keep checkouts byte-identical and diffs clean rather than to make the
# code work. Git stores text files with LF internally and applies the eol rules below on checkout.

# Let Git detect text files and normalize them, unless overridden below.
* text=auto

# PowerShell sources. Pinned to CRLF because the module is authored on Windows and its primary target,
# Windows PowerShell 5.1, is Windows-only. Pinning (rather than leaving it native) means a contributor on
# Linux or macOS gets the same bytes as one on Windows, so edits show up as real changes instead of a
# whole-file line-ending flip. PowerShell 7 on Linux and macOS reads CRLF without complaint.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.psm1   text eol=crlf
*.psd1   text eol=crlf
*.ps1xml text eol=crlf
*.psrc   text eol=crlf
*.pssc   text eol=crlf

# Docs, config, and data. Native line endings on checkout is fine for these.
*.md     text
*.txt    text
*.json   text
*.yml    text
*.yaml   text
*.url    text
*.csv    text
LICENSE  text

# Binaries. These must never be line-ending converted or treated as text: the bundled Microsoft WebView2
# assemblies under SKYAPI/Dependencies would be corrupted by any such conversion. Git's auto-detection
# normally catches these, but stating it removes the guesswork.
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.pdb    binary
*.nupkg  binary
*.zip    binary
*.docx   binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.snk    binary
*.pfx    binary
*.cer    binary
